## 20) Suggested self-guided exercises

_Story beat:_ Optional side quests to stress-test intuition and build muscle memory.

1. Change `config.effect.coefficient` and rerun impact + attribution. Watch how posterior uncertainty changes.
2. Add stronger seasonality to the synthetic generator and compare scalar vs structured impact recovery.
3. Replace `method: :mcmc` with `method: :filter` in intervention/anomaly sections and compare speed/accuracy.
4. Increase overlap in spots and compare exact/MC Shapley behavior (`n_samples`).
5. Add one irrelevant control series and see whether `CovariateSelection.select/3` excludes it.

## 21) End state checklist

_Story beat:_ Debrief complete. You now have a full campaign-measurement playbook from first signal to validated decision.

If you completed this notebook, you have built up the following statistical concepts layer by layer:

| Section | Statistical concept           | What you learned                                                                            |
| ------- | ----------------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| 3       | Data generating process       | Why synthetic data with known truth is essential for validating statistical methods         |
| 4       | Adstock + Hill saturation     | How marketing effects carry over in time and saturate with intensity                        |
| 5       | Kalman filtering              | Online Bayesian state estimation — the predict/update cycle that tracks latent state      |
| 6       | RTS smoothing                 | How conditioning on the full series (hindsight) improves latent state estimates             |
| 7       | Gibbs sampling / MCMC         | How to sample from a joint posterior when no closed-form solution exists                    |
| 8       | Structured state-space models | Composing trend, seasonality, and regression into an interpretable baseline model           |
| 9       | Causal impact                 | The counterfactual approach to causal inference — actual minus "what would have happened" |
| 11      | Covariate selection           | Screening controls to avoid overfitting while improving counterfactual accuracy             |
| 12      | Shapley values                | Fair credit assignment across overlapping interventions using cooperative game theory       |
| 14      | Bayesian forecasting          | Posterior predictive distributions with calibrated uncertainty intervals                    |
| 15      | Anomaly detection             | Using filter prediction errors as z-scores for statistical process control                  |
| 18      | Validation                    | Placebo tests, coverage checks, residual diagnostics, and calibration to build trust        |

That is the core and advanced statistical surface area of this library. Each section builds on the previous ones — the Kalman filter feeds the smoother, the smoother feeds the Gibbs sampler, the sampler feeds causal impact, and the validation checks close the loop by stress-testing the whole chain.
